# Lesson 13: Network Architecture (Peer-to-Peer and Client/Server)

> OBJ:
  - Explain network types based on the logical relationship between nodes.
  - Compare the advantages and disadvantages of each configuration.
  - Choose the appropriate network design for a given organisation.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Two ways to share notes: everyone photocopies from each other vs one library desk. Which is which? | Map to computing. |
| Core 1 | 30 min | Logical relationship vs physical layout; **peer-to-peer (P2P)**: all nodes equal, each shares/consumes; workgroup; examples (home sharing, small office, BitTorrent, blockchain); **client/server**: dedicated servers provide services to clients; domain; server types (file, print, web, mail, database, DHCP/DNS, application, authentication/directory); thin/thick clients; hybrid; cloud as client/server at scale. | Diagrams. |
| Core 2 | 30 min | Advantages/disadvantages: cost, set-up, administration, security, backup, scalability, performance, reliability/single point of failure, user limits (~10 for P2P). Demonstration: Windows workgroup share vs domain login (or Ubuntu Samba). | Comparison table; observe demo. |
| Core 3 | 27 min | Choosing a design: criteria (size, security, data centralisation, budget, skills, growth, applications); scenarios (home, 5-PC shop, 40-PC school lab, hospital, bank); hybrid choices (P2P lab printing + central file server); cloud-hosted servers. | Decide five cases. |
| Conclusion | 8 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 13.1 Network types by logical relationship between nodes

> DEF:
  **Network architecture** (logical design) describes **how nodes relate and share responsibility** – independent of cabling. In a **peer-to-peer (P2P) network** every node is equal: each can act as both **client** (requesting) and **server** (providing) resources. In a **client/server network** dedicated, more powerful **servers** provide services (files, printing, authentication, web, e-mail, databases) to **clients** that request them; management is centralised.

| Aspect | Peer-to-peer | Client/server |
| Roles | All equal; resources on individual PCs | Servers provide, clients consume |
| Management | Each user manages own machine, accounts and shares (**workgroup**) | Central administration – **domain**/directory (Active Directory, LDAP), policies |
| Authentication | Local accounts on each PC | Central accounts; single login everywhere |
| Typical size | 2–10 nodes | 10s to 100,000s |
| Examples | Home file/printer sharing, small office workgroup, LAN games, **BitTorrent**, blockchain networks, Wi-Fi Direct/AirDrop | School lab with a server, businesses, banks, web (browser ↔ web server), e-mail, cloud services, LMS |
| Server types (client/server) | – | **File**, **print**, **web**, **mail**, **database**, **application**, **DHCP/DNS**, **authentication/directory**, proxy/firewall, backup, media; physical, virtual or cloud |
| Clients | – | **Thick/fat** (full PCs running applications) vs **thin** (rely on server – terminals, Chromebooks, virtual desktops) |
| Hybrid | Common: central server for files/accounts while peers share printers or play LAN games | Peer-assisted delivery (Windows Update delivery optimisation, video CDNs) |

### 13.2 Advantages and disadvantages

| Criterion | Peer-to-peer | Client/server |
| **Cost** | Low – no server hardware/NOS licence | Higher – server, NOS/licences, possibly administrator |
| **Set-up & skills** | Simple; users can do it | Requires planning and skilled administration |
| **Administration** | Decentralised, becomes chaotic beyond ~10 PCs; each machine configured separately | Centralised accounts, policies, updates, monitoring |
| **Security** | Weak – each PC protects itself; shared passwords; no central logs | Strong – central authentication, permissions, auditing, policies |
| **Data & backup** | Scattered across machines; backups inconsistent | Centralised data; one backup point; version control |
| **Performance** | Fine for small loads; a PC acting as server slows for its user | Dedicated, optimised servers; scales with load |
| **Reliability** | No single point of failure, but resources vanish when a peer is off | Server is a single point of failure – mitigate with redundancy, UPS, backups |
| **Scalability** | Poor | Excellent |
| **Dependence** | Independent nodes | Clients depend on server and network |
| **Best for** | Homes, very small offices, temporary set-ups, decentralised apps | Organisations needing security, central data, many users |

### 13.3 Choosing the appropriate design

| Scenario | Choice | Reasoning |
| Family home: 2 laptops, printer, TV | **P2P** (Wi-Fi router, shared printer/folders) | Few nodes, low security needs, no admin |
| Tailoring shop with 4 PCs sharing files and a printer | **P2P workgroup** (perhaps a NAS for files) | Cheap; < 10 users |
| School lab: 40 PCs, learner accounts, shared folders, printing, LMS | **Client/server** (file/print/authentication server; maybe LMS server or cloud) | Central control, security, backups, many users |
| Hospital records | **Client/server** with redundancy | Sensitive data, availability |
| Bank | **Client/server** (data centre, thin clients in branches) | Security, control, scale |
| NGO with 6 laptops in different towns | **Cloud client/server** (Google Workspace/Microsoft 365) + P2P locally | No local server skills; mobility |
| LAN gaming tournament | **P2P** | Temporary, equal nodes |
| Video-sharing platform | **Client/server** with CDN/peer-assisted delivery | Scale |

Decision criteria: number of users/devices, security and privacy requirements, need for central data/backups, budget (capital + admin), available skills, growth, application requirements (databases need servers), reliability requirements, whether cloud can play the server role.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Lycée lab evolution.** Year 1: 20 PCs in a workgroup – learners saved work on random machines, shared folders had no passwords, a virus spread from a shared drive, printing worked only when "Poste 7" was on. Year 2: an Ubuntu server (Samba + LDAP, or Windows Server) provides personal home folders, class shares with permissions, central printing, nightly backup and one login per learner; the teacher deploys settings to all PCs at once. Cost: one refurbished server and a technician's week; benefit: order, security and time.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 13.1 – P2P vs client/server diagrams**: left, five PCs in a mesh of arrows all sharing; right, clients pointing to a server rack labelled file/print/auth/DHCP with an admin console. **Figure 13.2 – Comparison radar chart**: cost, security, scalability, simplicity, reliability plotted for each architecture. **Figure 13.3 – Decision flow**: users ≤ 10? sensitive data? central backup needed? skills/budget? → P2P / client-server / cloud-hosted.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Client/server needs a star topology and P2P a bus." Architecture is logical; both can run over the same star-wired switch. "A server is a special expensive machine." Any capable computer running server software can be a server; the role is what matters.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Build both (45 min, lab).** (1) P2P: create a workgroup share on two PCs (Windows: share a folder, set permissions; or Ubuntu: Samba share), access it from the other, print via a shared printer; note what happens when one PC is switched off. (2) Client/server: log in with domain/LDAP accounts if a server exists, or simulate with one PC acting as file server with user accounts; compare account management. (3) Role-play: 8 learners as peers passing "file requests" vs one "server" – time and chaos comparison. (4) Decide the architecture for five scenario cards with two reasons each.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Explain peer-to-peer and client/server architectures in terms of the logical relationship between nodes.
  2. Name six types of servers and their functions.
  3. Compare the two architectures on cost, security, administration, scalability and reliability.
  4. Why is P2P not recommended beyond about ten users?
  5. Choose and justify a design for: a home; a 6-PC pharmacy; a 500-student school; a hospital.
  6. Distinguish thin and thick clients; how does cloud computing relate to client/server?
  **Homework:** Draw the logical architecture of your school or a business you know, identify the servers (physical or cloud) and clients, and suggest one improvement.

---

# Lesson 14: Types of Physical Network Topologies

> OBJ:
  - Describe the types of physical network topologies (mesh, star, ring, bus, tree).
  - Identify the physical layout and connection pattern of each topology.
  - Explain the advantages and disadvantages of each topology.

## Lesson outline (2 periods, 100 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 5 min | Five learners hold strings in different patterns; class names the shapes. | Guess. |
| Core 1 | 45 min | Define physical vs logical topology; **bus** (single backbone, terminators, coax history, collisions), **star** (central switch/hub, UTP, dominant today), **ring** (each node to two neighbours, token passing, FDDI, dual ring), **mesh** (full vs partial, formula n(n−1)/2 links, WAN backbones, wireless mesh Wi-Fi systems), **tree/hierarchical** (star-of-stars, core–distribution–access, campus networks); also hybrid and point-to-point notes. For each: layout, connection pattern, cabling, how data travels, failure behaviour. | Draw each; count links. |
| Core 2 | 35 min | Advantages/disadvantages tables; cable length calculations; fault isolation exercises ("cut this link – who is affected?"). | Fault exercises. |
| Conclusion | 15 min | Build the star topology in the lab (patch cables to switch); exit ticket. | Practise. |

## Core content

### 14.1 Physical topology

> DEF:
  A **physical topology** is the **actual layout and connection pattern** of the cables, devices and nodes in a network. The **logical topology** is how data flows (e.g., a hub-based star behaves logically like a bus). The main physical topologies are **bus, star, ring, mesh and tree**.

| Topology | Layout & connection pattern | How data travels | Media/equipment | Where used |
| **Bus** | All nodes attach by drop lines/T-connectors to a **single backbone cable** with **terminators** at both ends | Signal broadcast along the backbone in both directions; every node hears it, only the addressed node accepts; collisions handled by CSMA/CD | Coaxial (10BASE2/10BASE5) historically; today in some industrial/ vehicle buses (CAN bus), and logically in shared Wi-Fi | Early LANs (1980s–90s); small temporary networks; teaching |
| **Star** | Every node connects by its **own cable to a central device** (switch, or hub historically); no node-to-node links | Sent to the central device, which forwards to the destination (switch) or broadcasts (hub) | UTP Cat 5e/6 with RJ-45, switch, patch panel; also Wi-Fi around an access point | **Dominant LAN topology** today: labs, offices, homes |
| **Ring** | Each node connects to **exactly two neighbours**, forming a closed loop | Data (often a **token**) travels in one direction node to node; each repeats the signal; **dual ring** (FDDI, SONET) adds a counter-rotating backup | Token Ring (IBM, twisted pair via MAU), FDDI (fibre), SONET/SDH metro rings | Legacy LANs; still in telecom/metro fibre rings and some industrial networks |
| **Mesh** | Every node connects to **every other** (**full mesh**: n(n−1)/2 links) or to several others (**partial mesh**) | Multiple paths; routing chooses the best; traffic re-routes around failures | Point-to-point links (fibre, leased lines, radio); routers; **wireless mesh** (home mesh Wi-Fi, community networks, IoT Zigbee) | Internet/WAN backbones, data centres, military, critical sites, mesh Wi-Fi |
| **Tree (hierarchical / extended star)** | **Stars connected to a central star** (or bus) in levels: **core → distribution → access** switches | Traffic climbs to the nearest common switch and descends | Fibre uplinks between switches, UTP to devices | Campus/school with several blocks, enterprises; most large LANs |
| Hybrid | Combination (e.g., star-ring, star-bus) | – | – | Real networks |
| Point-to-point | Two nodes directly linked | Direct | Cable/wireless bridge | Building-to-building links |

**Link counts:** star = n links (n nodes + 1 central device); ring = n; bus = 1 backbone + n drops; full mesh = n(n−1)/2 (10 nodes → 45 links; 40 nodes → 780); tree = n − 1 within the hierarchy.

### 14.2 Advantages and disadvantages

| Topology | Advantages | Disadvantages |
| **Bus** | Least cable; cheap and simple; easy to extend short distances; no central device | A backbone break or missing terminator **disables the whole network**; collisions limit performance as nodes increase; hard to troubleshoot; limited length and node count; security – all nodes see all traffic; obsolete for LANs |
| **Star** | **Easy to install, manage and troubleshoot**; a cable/node failure affects only that node; easy to add/remove nodes; high performance with a switch (dedicated bandwidth per port); centralised monitoring and security | **Central device is a single point of failure**; more cable than bus/ring; cost of switch and cabling; limited by switch ports and 100 m cable runs |
| **Ring** | Orderly access (token) – no collisions, predictable performance under load; each node regenerates the signal (long distances); equal access | A **single node/link failure can break the ring** (unless dual ring); adding/removing nodes disrupts the network; harder to troubleshoot; slower for small networks; legacy equipment |
| **Mesh** | **Highest reliability/fault tolerance** – multiple paths; no single point of failure; high performance (dedicated links); privacy/security per link; easy fault isolation | **Most expensive and complex** – links, ports and cabling grow quadratically; difficult to install and manage; impractical for large full-mesh LANs |
| **Tree** | **Scalable** for large sites; hierarchical management and segmentation (VLANs per block); fault in one branch isolated; uses standard star equipment; supports redundancy at the core | Depends on the **root/core** and uplinks; more cabling and switches; more complex design; distance/uplink bandwidth planning needed |

> EXAMPLE:
  **Topologies around one campus.** The lab is a **star** around a 48-port switch; the five blocks form a **tree** with fibre uplinks to a core switch in the admin block; the ISP's metro fibre is a **dual ring** through the city; the internet backbone linking Douala, Paris and Lagos is a **partial mesh**; the industrial workshop's machine controllers still use a **bus** (CAN); the home mesh Wi-Fi kit a teacher bought forms a wireless **mesh**.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 14.1 – Five topology diagrams** side by side (bus with terminators, star with switch, ring with token arrow, full mesh with link count, tree with core/distribution/access levels), each annotated with "failure of X affects…". **Figure 14.2 – Cable-length comparison** for 8 nodes in a 10 m × 10 m room under each topology. **Figure 14.3 – Photo/sketch of a real star**: patch panel, switch, cables to desks.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Wi-Fi has no topology." Wireless LANs are logically bus-like (shared medium) around an access point in a star; mesh Wi-Fi is a mesh. "Star is fragile because of the switch." Switches are extremely reliable; redundancy is possible; per-node isolation makes star the most manageable.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Topology lab (45 min).** (1) String-and-people models of all five topologies; the teacher "cuts" a link – who is isolated? (2) Lab practice: connect five PCs to a switch (star) with patch cables, verify link lights and ping; then daisy-chain two switches (tree) and repeat. (3) Calculations: links for 6, 12 and 30 nodes in full mesh; cable totals for star vs bus in a given room plan. (4) Packet Tracer (if available): build each topology and simulate a failure.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Describe bus, star, ring, mesh and tree topologies with a diagram of each.
  2. Identify the topology from descriptions: "each node connects to two neighbours"; "all nodes to one switch"; "stars connected to a central switch"; "single backbone with terminators".
  3. Explain two advantages and two disadvantages of each topology.
  4. How many links does a full mesh of 12 nodes need? Why is full mesh impractical for LANs?
  5. What happens in bus, star and ring topologies when one cable breaks?
  6. Why is star the dominant LAN topology and where are ring and mesh still used?
  **Homework:** Sketch the physical topology of your school lab or a cybercafé, label devices and cables, name the topology and propose one improvement for reliability.

---

# Lesson 15: Comparing and Choosing Network Topologies

> OBJ:
  - Compare the different types of physical network topologies.
  - Evaluate which topology suits specific organisational needs.
  - Propose a suitable network topology for a given organisation.

## Lesson outline (1 period, 50 min)

| Phase | Time | Teacher activity | Learner activity |
| Introduction | 4 min | "A clinic asks for the most reliable topology at the lowest cost – contradiction?" | Discuss. |
| Core 1 | 16 min | Comparison matrix: cost, cabling, installation, scalability, reliability/fault tolerance, performance, troubleshooting, security, suitability; scoring. | Complete matrix. |
| Core 2 | 16 min | Evaluation criteria for organisations (size, layout/building, budget, reliability requirement, growth, existing cabling, expertise, application traffic); matching topology to needs; hybrid proposals; documenting a proposal (floor plan, device list, cable schedule, justification). | Evaluate three scenarios. |
| Core 3 | 10 min | Group proposals for given organisation. | Draft proposal. |
| Conclusion | 4 min | Exit ticket. | Answer. |

## Core content

### 15.1 Comparison matrix

| Criterion | Bus | Star | Ring | Mesh | Tree |
| **Cabling cost** | Lowest | Moderate | Moderate | Highest | Moderate–high |
| **Equipment cost** | Low (no central device) | Switch(es) | MAUs/legacy | Many ports/routers | Several switches |
| **Ease of installation** | Easy (small) | Easy | Moderate | Hard | Moderate |
| **Scalability** | Poor | Good (add ports/switches) | Poor (disruptive) | Very poor for full mesh | **Excellent** |
| **Reliability / fault tolerance** | Poor – backbone failure kills all | Good – node faults isolated; switch is SPOF | Poor unless dual ring | **Excellent** | Good – branch faults isolated; core is SPOF |
| **Performance under load** | Degrades (collisions) | High (switched) | Predictable, moderate | Highest | High, depends on uplinks |
| **Troubleshooting** | Hard | **Easy** | Hard | Easy per link, complex overall | Moderate |
| **Security (traffic isolation)** | Low (shared medium) | High with switches | Low–moderate | High | High (VLANs) |
| **Typical size** | Small | Small–medium | Small–medium | Small core/WAN | Medium–large |
| **Current relevance** | Legacy/industrial | **Standard LAN** | Telecom rings | Backbones, Wi-Fi mesh | Campus/enterprise |

### 15.2 Evaluating suitability for organisational needs

| Need / constraint | Favoured topology | Comment |
| Single room, ≤ 48 devices, limited budget | **Star** | Simple, manageable |
| Several buildings or floors, growth expected | **Tree** (extended star with fibre uplinks) | Scalable, segmentable |
| Mission-critical availability (hospital core, bank data centre, ISP backbone) | **Mesh** (partial) at the core; **dual ring** for metro links | Redundant paths |
| Temporary or very small ad-hoc set-up | Bus-like/Wi-Fi ad hoc or small star | Quick |
| Wireless coverage of a large house/compound without cabling | **Wireless mesh** | Self-healing coverage |
| Industrial machines/sensors | Fieldbus (bus) or ring for determinism | Legacy compatibility |
| Easy troubleshooting with few technicians | **Star/tree** | Per-port diagnosis |
| Minimal cable in a long corridor of few devices | Historically bus; today small star per zone | – |

Proposal method: (1) collect requirements (devices per room, rooms/buildings, distances, growth, budget, reliability, applications); (2) map the site (floor plan, cable routes, distances ≤ 90 m for UTP); (3) choose topology per level (access star, campus tree, redundant core); (4) list equipment (switch ports + 20 % spare, cabling, patch panels, uplinks, UPS); (5) justify against criteria; (6) draw diagram and cable schedule; (7) estimate cost and phases.

> EXAMPLE:
  **Proposal for "Clinique La Grâce"** (two floors, 25 PCs, imaging machine, Wi-Fi tablets, 24/7 operation). Access layer: **star** per floor (24-port PoE switch each, UTP Cat 6, access points); distribution: a **tree** joining floor switches to a core switch by two fibre uplinks each (partial mesh at the core for redundancy); imaging on its own VLAN; UPS on all switches. Rejected: full mesh (cost), ring (disruption on changes), bus (fragile). Result: star-tree hybrid with redundant core — reliability where it matters, economy elsewhere.

> VISUAL:
  **Figure 15.1 – Heat-map comparison matrix** (green/amber/red cells) of the five topologies against criteria. **Figure 15.2 – Proposal drawing**: clinic floor plan with switch locations, cable runs, fibre uplinks, redundant core links, VLAN colours. **Figure 15.3 – Decision flow**: size → buildings → reliability need → budget → topology recommendation.

> MISCONCEPTION:
  "Choose one topology for the whole organisation." Real designs are hybrids: star at the edge, tree across the site, mesh/ring at the core. "Mesh is always the best because it is most reliable." Its cost and complexity are justified only where downtime is intolerable.

> ACTIVITY:
  **Topology proposal (35 min, groups).** Each group gets an organisation card with floor plan (primary school with 2 blocks; cybercafé; council office with 3 floors; radio station needing 24/7 uptime). Produce: comparison scoring for three candidate topologies against the organisation's top five criteria, a labelled topology drawing with equipment list and cable schedule, and a justified proposal presented in 3 minutes; class votes on the most convincing.

## Formative assessment

> ASSESS:
  1. Compare star, ring, bus, mesh and tree topologies on cost, scalability, reliability and troubleshooting.
  2. Which topology has no single point of failure, and at what cost?
  3. Evaluate which topology suits: a 30-PC lab; a hospital core network; a house with poor Wi-Fi coverage; a lycée with four blocks.
  4. Propose and justify a topology for a bank branch with 15 PCs, two ATMs and a requirement of high availability.
  5. Why do real networks combine topologies? Give an example.
  **Homework:** Prepare a one-page topology proposal (drawing + justification + equipment list) for a small business of your choice.

---

# Lesson 16: Integration Activities — Designing the Architecture and Topology of an Organisation's Network

> ACTIVITY:
  **Context.** A new bilingual secondary school opens next year with: an administration block (10 PCs, server room), two classroom blocks (each with a 30-PC lab and staff Wi-Fi), a library (8 PCs + Wi-Fi) and a boarding house 150 m away needing supervised evening Wi-Fi. Data: 1,200 learners, 80 staff, growth to 1,800 in three years.
  **Task (groups of 3–4, 2 periods).**
  1. **Architecture** (Lesson 13): choose peer-to-peer, client/server or hybrid for the whole school and for each area; specify the servers/services (file, print, authentication, DHCP/DNS, LMS – on-premises or cloud) and client types; justify with advantages/disadvantages.
  2. **Topologies** (Lessons 14–15): propose the physical topology per block and for the campus (star/tree/mesh/ring elements), with a comparison table of at least three alternatives against the school's criteria (cost, reliability, scalability, troubleshooting, security), link counts and cable-length estimates, and the treatment of the 150 m boarding-house link (fibre vs wireless bridge).
  3. **Drawings**: campus topology diagram and one detailed block diagram (switches, ports, uplinks, access points, server room).
  4. **Equipment list and phased budget** (year 1 essentials, year 2–3 growth).
  5. **Presentation** (6 min) to a "school board" panel; answer questions on failure scenarios ("what if the core switch fails?").
  **Criteria.** Sound architecture with justified services; appropriate topology choices with quantitative comparison; correct, legible drawings; realistic equipment/budget; failure-scenario reasoning; presentation.

## Rubric

| Criterion | 4 – Excellent | 3 – Good | 2 – Developing | 1 – Beginning |
| Architecture & services | Correct choice, complete server/service plan, strong justification | Minor gaps | Partial | Inappropriate |
| Topology comparison & choice | ≥ 3 alternatives compared quantitatively; hybrid design well argued | Compared; adequate | Limited | Unjustified |
| Diagrams | Clear campus and block diagrams with ports/uplinks/APs | Clear | Incomplete | Missing |
| Equipment & budget | Realistic, phased, includes spares/UPS | Realistic | Rough | Absent |
| Resilience reasoning | Failure scenarios answered convincingly | Mostly | Weak | None |
| Presentation | Professional, shared roles | Clear | Uneven | Weak |

